  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider an electrical circuit containing a two way switch <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Initially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> is open and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is connected to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;As the current in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> attains a maximum value of steady-state level, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is disconnected from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and immediately connected to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;Potential drop across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistor immediately after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is connected to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>.</mo></math> (Round off to the Nearest Integer)</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0080_1f7d9ddcba93.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 139px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are connected, then the steady state current in the inductor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are connected then current through inductor remains same. So potential difference across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p>"
  }
}